# ShadowMerchant — Production Deep Audit Report

> **Audit Date:** May 16, 2026  
> **Codebase:** `apps/web` (Next.js 16.2.1 / React 19 / App Router)  
> **Deployment:** Vercel (bom1 region) + MongoDB Atlas + Upstash Redis  
> **Files Analyzed:** ~70 source files across API routes, pages, components, models, and config

---

## Executive Summary

The ShadowMerchant codebase is architecturally sound — clean Next.js App Router structure, proper Clerk auth integration, robust Redis caching with fallbacks, and well-indexed MongoDB schemas. However, the audit uncovered **22 issues** that will cause errors or degraded behavior in live deployment. **5 are build-breaking or crash-level blockers** that must be fixed before any production push.

---

## 🔴 CRITICAL — Build Breakers & Crash-Level (P0)

### C-01: Missing `middleware.ts` — Clerk Auth Is Completely Bypassed

| Field | Detail |
|-------|--------|
| **File** | `src/proxy.ts` (exists) vs `middleware.ts` (MISSING) |
| **Impact** | Auth protection for `/dashboard`, `/wishlist`, `/alerts`, and `/admin` routes is **non-functional** |

**Root Cause:** The Clerk middleware logic exists in `src/proxy.ts`, but Next.js requires the middleware file to be at the project root as `middleware.ts` (or `src/middleware.ts` inside the `src/` directory). The file is named `proxy.ts` — Next.js **will never execute it**. No `middleware.ts` file exists anywhere in the project.

**Result:** 
- Any unauthenticated user can access `/dashboard`, `/wishlist`, `/alerts` pages
- Any non-admin user can access `/admin/*` — the admin layout has a server-side check via `sessionClaims`, but the middleware-level redirect never fires, meaning the page partially renders before redirecting
- Protected API routes that rely on `auth()` still work (they check auth server-side), but the UX is broken — users see flash of content before redirect

**Fix:** Rename `src/proxy.ts` → `src/middleware.ts`

